+++ b/openspec/changes/archive/2026-10-22-fix-patch-rfc6902-conformance/design.md
@@ -0,0 +1,8 @@
+## Context
+
+`replace` currently calls `removeAt` then `addAt`, which deletes and re-creates the object key.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- Implement `replace` by resolving the parent with `parentOf` and assigning the member (`setMember`) or array slot directly. Root replacement is unchanged.
+- No other behaviour changes: review of array indices, move/copy, test equality and atomicity found them conforming.
diff --git a/openspec/changes/archive/2026-10-22-fix-patch-rfc6902-conformance/proposal.md b/openspec/changes/archive/2026-10-22-fix-patch-rfc6902-conformance/proposal.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..fe3c80a554a4d38d6a333f2a7c0711c78d244ff5
--- /dev/null
+++ b/openspec/changes/archive/2026-10-22-fix-patch-rfc6902-conformance/proposal.md
@@ -0,0 +1,20 @@
+## Why
+
+Users report that `applyPatch` does not always behave as RFC 6902 reads. A review against the RFC found that `replace` is implemented as remove + add, so it relocates an existing object member to the end of the key order instead of replacing the value at that location (RFC 6902 §4.3).
+
+## What Changes
+
+- `replace` sets the value in place (object member keeps its position; array element is overwritten without shifting).
+- Re-verify array index handling, move/copy, test equality and atomic failure against the RFC, and add tests pinning each.
+
+## Capabilities
+
+### New Capabilities
+(none)
+
+### Modified Capabilities
+- `json-patch`: `replace` must replace in place.
+
+## Impact
+
+`src/index.ts` (`applyPatch`), `test/patch.test.ts`.
